{"body":"Trail: https://entire.io/gh/MuskanPaliwal/cli/trails/12\n\nClaude Code 2.1.278 exposed a bad assumption in the review adapter. Entire treated every top-level `message` field as an assistant message object, but Claude uses different message shapes for different event types.\n\nA valid `permission_denied` system event carries `message` as a string:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"system\",\n  \"subtype\": \"permission_denied\",\n  \"message\": \"Permission denied\"\n}\n```\n\nEntire tried to decode that string into `claudeMessage` and returned:\n\n```text\njson: cannot unmarshal string into Go struct field\nclaudeEnvelope.message of type claudecode.claudeMessage\n```\n\nThe parser kept reading, so Claude's review still appeared in the terminal. The error had already marked the Claude run as failed. Entire then dropped Claude's report from the final review.\n\nThat exposed a second bug. When Codex was the only successful reviewer and returned a clean approval, Entire posted the raw response as a severity-less whole-change finding. The Trail displayed it as \"Unspecified finding.\"\n\n## What changed\n\nThe Claude envelope now stores `message` as raw JSON. The adapter checks the event type before decoding it.\n\nAssistant events still decode into `claudeMessage`, so text, tool calls, message IDs, and token usage work as before. Malformed assistant messages still produce `RunError`.\n\nSystem events no longer have to match the assistant schema. A valid event such as `permission_denied` can pass through without failing the review.\n\nThe Trail conversion now treats a clean approval as review metadata rather than a finding. It recognizes plain approvals, punctuation, Markdown emphasis, Unicode separators, and short reasons.\n\nThe check runs only when the output has no structured comments or Markdown finding bullets. Real findings still win.\n\n`approve with nits` and `request changes` still produce findings. Words such as `approved` do not count as an `approve` verdict.\n\n## Why the old tests passed\n\nThe Claude parser tests covered assistant messages, results, malformed JSON, missing results, and scanner failures. They did not include a valid system event with a string-valued `message`.\n\nThe Trail test explicitly expected `approve - no actionable findings` to create one whole-change finding. The test locked in the behavior that produced \"Unspecified finding.\"\n\nThe new tests cover the missing protocol shape and replace the old clean-verdict expectation.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe Claude regression test sends a `permission_denied` event, assistant text, and a successful result through the parser. It confirms that the system event does not emit `RunError`, the assistant text survives, and the run finishes successfully.\n\nA separate test passes a string as an assistant event's `message`. That still emits `RunError`, so this change does not make malformed assistant output valid.\n\nTrail tests cover standalone approvals, the Codex fallback format, punctuation, Markdown emphasis, plain-language reasons, nits, request changes, and non-verdict words such as `approved`.\n\nThe branch is based on the current fork and upstream `main`.\n\n- `mise run fmt` passes.\n- `mise run lint` passes with 0 issues.\n- `mise run test` passes all 11,558 tests, with 10 skipped.\n- The race run passes every package except one unrelated signal-forwarding test. That test passes on an immediate focused rerun.\n- Both E2E canaries pass, with 56 Vogon tests and 4 Roger Roger tests.\n- `git diff --check` passes.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis PR does not change Claude permissions, review prompts, reviewer failure policy, synthesis rules, Trail API types, or server rendering.\n\nIt fixes the two client-side decisions that turned a valid Claude event and a clean review into a failed agent and an \"Unspecified finding.\"\n","checks":[{"conclusion":"NEUTRAL","name":"Entire Gates"}],"comments":[],"reviewDecision":"REVIEW_REQUIRED"}

